 
          <p>Episode 800 of the Retronauts podcasts <a href="https://retronauts.com/article/2568/retronauts-episode-800-20-years-of-retronauts">released recently</a>, celebrating twenty years of floating around in the podcast-o-sphere. <em>Twenty</em> years of making podcasts and <em>eight hundred</em> episodes, not counting the ones Jeremy and co. did for <em>1UP</em>! That&rsquo;s a remarkable achievement considering most other podcasts I listen(ed) to only record one, perhaps two to three seasons and that&rsquo;s it.</p>
<p>I don&rsquo;t think I need to advertise <em>Retronauts</em> or the work of Jeremy Parish and his merry band of co-hosts. So why this write-up? Because, like Jeremy mentioned in the episode, after Nadia says that games keep on getting older:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That means I have something in common with [these] games.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Given that I am of the more or less same vintage year of birth as most people in the show, that fact stings and lingers here as well. Time and life marches on, I write a few silly quips on a yearly basis <a href="/post/2026/08/forty-one/">on my birthday</a>, and that&rsquo;s it. But now and then, triggered by these special events, the sudden weight of forty-plus reappears. Not necessarily in a bad and depressing way&mdash;although that perhaps as well&mdash;but in a surprising way that I didn&rsquo;t consider before. It&rsquo;s hard to explain.</p>
<p>I&rsquo;ve been with Retronauts since day one. Actually, since day minus one. I fondly remember downloading the latest <em>1UP Show</em> on Friday evening which took a while considering the 2005 internet connection speeds. I would try to make up an excuse to race to my bedroom as quickly as possible after dinner to go watch the show accompanied by a can of Pepsi Twist, where my hero Jeremy would defend the weird Nintendo DS titles everyone else discarded in favour of the big AAA titles. Evidence of that can, including very modern flatron CRT and generic beige ergonomic keyboard, is immortalised in a photo on <a href="/post/2026/04/my-workspaces/">the My Workspaces blog post</a>.</p>
<p>Unfortunately those magical moments didn&rsquo;t last long as Ziff Davis sold <em>1UP</em> to UGO, effectively ending the show vodcast/podcast publications as well. And then there was the IGN crisis and then in 2013 the whole thing came crashing down. A year later, <em>Retronauts</em> emerged from the ashes, thanks to a Kickstarter to cover bootstrapping costs and Patreon to keep the show independent ever since. Never sell your business unless you want to take the money and run with it. Oh hi EA, how&rsquo;s BioWare going lately? Another graveyard? Oh.</p>
<p>Here&rsquo;s Jeremy in episode 4 of <em>The 1UP Show</em> (Oct 2005) discussing <em>Mario Kart DS</em> outside the office with then news editor Jane Pinckard. Conclusion? This one&rsquo;s better than <em>Double Dash</em>. Remember when people brought their DSes out in the open to race against each other? Remember when mobile gaming was still mobile? The Switch (2) and Steam Deck aren&rsquo;t exactly pocketable nowadays.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/10/twenty-years-of-retronauts/1upshow.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/10/twenty-years-of-retronauts/1upshow.jpg" loading="lazy" title="Two people holding a DS in front of a corporate building signalling &#39;hey, you can play these games outside!&#39;" data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>Two people holding a DS in front of a corporate building signalling &#39;hey, you can play these games outside!&#39;</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Jeremy: I really hate the new blue shell which flies towards you. <br/>Jane: Why do you hate it? <br/>Jeremy: Because it then drops on you and explodes and then flips you over and there&rsquo;s no way to dodge it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The most annoying power-up since its inception in this DS installment.</p>
<p>Depending on the person you&rsquo;d ask, <em>Mario Kart DS</em>&mdash;and to an extend, most DS games&mdash;were already &ldquo;retro&rdquo; when they got released. I&rsquo;m so glad Jeremy kept digging himself deeper into the retro gaming scene, resulting in the invention of the word &ldquo;metroidvania&rdquo;, the very successful <em>Retronauts</em> podcast, his recent work at Limited Run Games, and of course the YouTube &ldquo;[platform] Works&rdquo; series where he discusses the history and context of each and every retro game on multiple platforms, such as his last video posted only 22 hours ago on <em>Haunted Castle</em> for <em>NES Works</em>:</p>
<figure>
	<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=85M-rWbaeis" class="lbox"><img loading="lazy" class="img-responsive" src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/10/twenty-years-of-retronauts/yt-85M-rWbaeis.jpg" title="YouTube video 85M-rWbaeis">
	</a>
	</figure>

<p>The older we get, the more ground there is to cover in the world of retro gaming! The original <em>Castlevania</em> celebrated its fortieth birthday earlier this year. Perhaps all these dates and events are related in some way?</p>
<p>I just can&rsquo;t believe that twenty years later, I am still playing these games, listening to these shows, following the new old related news. Yet so much has changed: marriage<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>, children, jobs, houses. So too did Retronauts evolve yet consistently kept on delivering retro gaming goodness to our ears. In the 800rd episode, they discuss its history and gradual movement from in-person recording in the San Francisco Bay Area&mdash;when gaming journalism was still a viable option there&mdash;to everyone moving out and primarily recording online, further necessitated by a certain global pandemic.</p>
<p>The celebration of Twenty years of Retronauts for me triggers questions such as <em>have I made the right decisions in these last twenty years?</em> and <em>will I be privileged to still be here after the next twenty years?</em> I am no longer that Pepsi Twist gulper that wastes most of his time playing games and tinkering with the Linux kernel. I sometimes miss that person. I wonder what he wanted to get out of life. The answer is probably nothing because at that age you don&rsquo;t ask yourself stupid questions like that. If you are reading this and you are in your twenties, please do yourself a favour and <em>do</em> ask yourself these questions.</p>
<p>2005 was also the year I started working on a site called <em>Jefklak&rsquo;s Codex</em>, <a href="https://jefklakscodex.com/about/">the fourth attempt</a> at creating a gaming-related site. The most popular article was the my experimental DS DOOM port on the DS Xtreme card.</p>
<p>Thank you ToastyFrog and co., for your perseverance on all things obscure pixels. You were and are a beacon of inspiration; here&rsquo;s to another 800. I hope by then my retro cartridge collection isn&rsquo;t disintegrated in some way. I hope by then I myself am not disintegrated in some way.</p>
<p>Other than the unstoppable ravaging effects of another twenty years of time added to the clock, that is.</p>
